/*
	Order of the hot code at the start of the executable (`--section-ordering-file`, see the Nintendo 64 arm
	of ncine_extra_sources.cmake).

	The VR4300's instruction cache is 16 KB and direct-mapped: two lines of code 16 KB apart (or any multiple of
	it) share a cache line and evict each other. The code a frame runs is some 400 KB, so it never fits, and
	what matters is which of it shares lines: functions that call each other in a loop and happen to collide
	refetch their code on every iteration. Left to the link order, where a function ends up relative to the ones
	it works with is an accident that any change anywhere reshuffles - the "code layout" swing of 10-20% that
	unrelated changes used to cause.

	So the functions that 99% of a frame's time is spent in are placed here, in an order made from a profile
	rather than by hand: a timer interrupt sampled the program counter, its caller and the phase of the frame
	through a dozen scenes of six levels (standing, shooting, the weapon wheel open). Every function is placed
	with the phase most of its samples fall in, as the phases run one after another and so the code of one never
	competes with the code of another; within a phase, functions that call each other are chained together (up
	to 4 KB a chain, so that a chain and what it calls into still fit the cache), and the chains follow in the
	order of how much time is spent per byte of them, which puts the inner loops first. Against the hand-made
	order this replaced (the loops of the RDP dispatch, the actor update, the collisions and the mixer, about
	half of the samples) the same build ran 3 to 17% faster in every one of 36 measured scenes, 10% on average.

	Sections are matched by their full mangled name, so a function that is renamed or whose signature changes
	silently drops out of the order (it is then placed by the link order again, as everything not listed here
	is), and code that became hot since is not in it - the order is worth making again from a new profile when
	the frame has changed a lot.
*/
